O THE ACTUAL CHANGE — between 5 and 30 subagents, never fewer than 5, never more than 30. Start from the five REVIEW BAR dimensions: a small, focused PR gets ~5, one subagent per dimension. For a larger or multi-module change, SPLIT each dimension across the changed files/areas/logic-branches so no subagent reviews more than it can hold deeply, scaling the count up toward 30 as the diff's size, file count, and branch complexity grow. Each subagent scrutinizes only its assigned slice through its lens and returns concrete candidate findings, each tied to a specific file, branch, and condition. ROUND 2 (confirmation) — ALWAYS run it, never skip it: treat every candidate finding as unproven and dispatch confirmation subagents to independently re-verify each one (re-read the exact code path or re-run the specific test); for anything you would block on, confirm it a second time before you trust it. Discard whatever you cannot reproduce; a finding earns its place in the report and the verdict only once confirmed. You own the synthesis — merge the surviving findings, drop duplicates, and write the single report.\n\n**EVIDENCE & HONESTY** — Report only what you observed. Run the tests; state the exact command and result; never claim a test passed that you did not run, never invent a finding. Treat CI exactly as the checks report it: red OR still-pending CI is a BLOCKING problem — confirm it with `gh pr checks \"$BRANCH\" --repo $OWNER/$REPO`, never assume green. If a state is unknown, say so and treat it as not-yet-ready.\n\nMANDATORY OUTPUT — write /agent/output/summary.md (absolute path) with EXACTLY these headers, in this order, and keep the whole file to 6000 characters or fewer (a later workflow step posts it VERBATIM as a task comment, and a toolless judge reads ONLY this file to decide merge-readiness — it must stand fully on its own):\n## Summary\none-line verdict and what you reviewed.\n## Pull request\nthe canonical PR number + URL.\n## Tests\nthe exact test/build command you ran and its result.\n## CI\nthe CI state (checks passed/failed/pending) — never assume green.\nEnd the file with EXACTLY one verdict line, worded so it can't be misread: \"READY TO MERGE\" — only when the bar is met, tests pass, and CI is confirmed green; or \"NOT READY — CHANGES REQUIRED\" followed by a numbered list of the specific blocking points, each naming the file and branch/condition to fix. Be decisive — never soften an unresolved problem into an approval, never hedge a clear pass.",
